<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=dam">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3000 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A dam is a structure that impounds water or restricts its flow. The earliest known dam is the Jawa Dam, built around 3000 BCE near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a> in Jordan, an embankment dam 28 m thick and 5.5 m high that was part of a water supply system. Around 2600 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> built the Sadd el-Kafara embankment dam near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, which failed around the time it was completed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> built several dams between the 17th and 13th centuries BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> began building masonry gravity dams in the 1st century CE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib-dam">Great Dam of Marib</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> was built around 500 BCE. The Kurit Dam in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, built around 1350 CE, was the first large thin arch dam.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dam.jpg" alt="Dam" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wiki Commons users Qurren at · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Takato_Dam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
